/* book Worked example: Q: An RC low-pass filter has R = 1 kΩ and C = 100 nF. (a) Find fc. (b) Find gain and phase at 1 kHz. (c) A series RLC band-pass has f₀ = 1 kHz and Q = 10: find its bandwidth and its gain at 1.2 kHz.
   1. Convert: R = 1 kΩ = 1000 Ω, C = 100 nF = 100 × 10⁻⁹ F, f = 1 kHz = 1000 Hz.
   2. Cutoff: fc = 1/(2πRC) = 1/(2π × 1000 × 100 × 10⁻⁹) = 1/(6.2832 × 10⁻⁴) = 1591.5 Hz.
   3. Frequency ratio: f/fc = 1000/1591.5 = 0.6283.
   4. Gain: |H| = 1/√(1 + 0.6283²) = 1/√(1.3948) = 1/1.1810 = 0.8467 ≈ 0.847.
   5. Phase: φ = −arctan(0.6283) = −32.14° ≈ −32.1°.
   6. RLC bandwidth: BW = f₀/Q = 1000/10 = 100 Hz. Approximate half-power points: 950 Hz and 1050 Hz.
   7. Gain at 1.2 kHz: ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω = 1.2 − 1/1.2 = 0.3667; Q × 0.3667 = 3.667; |H| = 1/√(1 + 3.667²) = 1/3.801 = 0.263, about −11.6 dB.
   Answer: fc ≈ 1591.5 Hz; at 1 kHz the gain is 0.847 (−1.44 dB) with phase −32.1°; the band-pass has BW = 100 Hz and passes only 0.263 of the input at 1.2 kHz. */
