/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take the supply voltage V as the reference phasor, along the positive real axis.
   - The current is V times the inverse of that line, and a straight line that misses the origin inverts into a circle through the origin.
   - To draw a current locus in an exam:
   - Series RL with R varied and XL fixed.
   - |I| = (V/XL) sin θ (R varied), |I| = (V/R) cos θ (XL varied)
   - Here θ is the angle between I and V; the first is a circle of diameter V/XL on the −90° axis, the second a circle of diameter V/R on the V axis.
   - Series RL with XL varied and R fixed.
   - In every case the diameter is V over the fixed element, and the 45° point is where the varied element equals the fixed one.
   - In a series RLC circuit with R fixed, the net reactance X = ωL − 1/(ωC) runs from −∞ to +∞ as ω rises from 0 to ∞.
   - In a parallel circuit the branch currents add.
*/
