/* book Worked example: Q: A series RL circuit has XL = 10 Ω and a variable resistor R, fed from 100 V. Draw the current locus, find the current for R = 0, 5, 10, 20 and 40 Ω, and find the value of R for maximum power and that power.
   1. Fixed element XL = 10 Ω, so the diameter is V/XL = 100/10 = 10 A, along the −90° axis. Centre at 5 A below the origin.
   2. R = 0: I = 100/(j10) = 10∠−90° A, the bottom of the circle.
   3. R = 5 Ω: I = 100/(5 + j10) = 100/11.18∠63.4° = 8.94∠−63.4° A = 4 − j8 A.
   4. R = 10 Ω: I = 100/(10 + j10) = 7.07∠−45° A = 5 − j5 A, the right-most point.
   5. R = 20 Ω: I = 4.47∠−26.6° A = 4 − j2 A. R = 40 Ω: I = 2.43∠−14.0° A.
   6. Check that the points lie on the circle: 4 − j8 is √(4² + 3²) = 5 A from the centre (0, −5); so are 5 − j5 and 4 − j2.
   7. Power P = V × Re(I) is largest at the right-most point, R = XL = 10 Ω: P = 100 × 5 = 500 W = V²/(2XL).
   Answer: The locus is a semicircle of diameter 10 A below V; the currents are 10, 8.94, 7.07, 4.47 and 2.43 A; maximum power 500 W at R = 10 Ω. */
