/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A current i through a coil sets up a magnetic flux.
   - For a linear magnetic core, L is constant.
   - L = NΦ/i, |e| = L |di/dt|
   - Here l is self-inductance in henry (H); N is turns; Φ is flux per turn in weber (Wb); i is current in ampere (A); e is self-induced emf in volt (V); di/dt is current change rate in A/s.
   - Put coil 2 near coil 1.
   - The induced emf in coil 2 is e₂ = −M di₁/dt; its magnitude is |e₂| = M |di₁/dt|.
   - M = N₂Φ₂₁/i₁, |e₂| = M |di₁/dt|
   - Here m is mutual inductance in henry (H); N₂ is turns of coil 2; Φ₂₁ is flux from coil 1 linking coil 2 in weber (Wb); i₁ is coil 1 current in ampere (A); e₂ is emf in coil 2 in volt (V); di₁/dt is coil 1 current change rate in A/s.
   - M alone does not tell how well two coils are coupled: a big coil can have a big M even with poor sharing.
   - Flux from coil 1 that does not link coil 2 is leakage flux Φℓ1.
*/
