/* book exam traps: M is not k(L₁ + L₂) and not the average of L₁ and L₂; use M = k√(L₁L₂). k is dimensionless and cannot be greater than 1. | Mutual voltage in coil 2 uses the current change in coil 1. Coil 2's own current creates its own self-emf, not its mutual emf from coil 1. | M can be zero even when the coils are physically close: perpendicular axes, cancellation of flux, or a shield can leave no net mutual flux. | Unit slips: L and M are in henry (H); k has no unit; in |e| = M |di/dt| the current rate must be in A/s, not A. | k = 1 means all flux links both coils. Real iron-core devices can be close to this, but leakage flux prevents a perfect 1 in most practical coils. */
